NNAAMMEE
       pivot_root - change the root filesystem

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppiivvoott__rroooott _n_e_w___r_o_o_t _p_u_t___o_l_d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ppiivvoott__rroooott moves the root file system of the current process to the
       directory _p_u_t___o_l_d and makes _n_e_w___r_o_o_t the new root file system. Since
       ppiivvoott__rroooott(8) simply calls ppiivvoott__rroooott(2), we refer to the man page of
       the latter for further details.

       Note that, depending on the implementation of ppiivvoott__rroooott, root and
       current working directory of the caller may or may not change. The
       following is a sequence for invoking ppiivvoott__rroooott that works in either
       case, assuming that ppiivvoott__rroooott and cchhrroooott are in the current PPAATTHH:

           cd new_root
           pivot_root . put_old
           exec chroot . command

       Note that cchhrroooott must be available under the old root and under the new
       root, because ppiivvoott__rroooott may or may not have implicitly changed the
       root directory of the shell.

       Note that eexxeecc cchhrroooott changes the running executable, which is
       necessary if the old root directory should be unmounted afterwards.
       Also note that standard input, output, and error may still point to a
       device on the old root file system, keeping it busy. They can easily be
       changed when invoking cchhrroooott (see below; note the absence of leading
       slashes to make it work whether ppiivvoott__rroooott has changed the shell’s root
       or not).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
       Change the root file system to _/_d_e_v_/_h_d_a_1 from an interactive shell:

           mount /dev/hda1 /new-root
           cd /new-root
           pivot_root . old-root
           exec chroot . sh <dev/console >dev/console 2>&1
           umount /old-root

       Mount the new root file system over NFS from 10.0.0.1:/my_root and run
       iinniitt:

           ifconfig lo 127.0.0.1 up   # for portmap
           # configure Ethernet or such
           portmap   # for lockd (implicitly started by mount)
           mount -o ro 10.0.0.1:/my_root /mnt
           killall portmap   # portmap keeps old root busy
           cd /mnt
           pivot_root . old_root
           exec chroot . sh -c 'umount /old_root; exec /sbin/init' \
             <dev/console >dev/console 2>&1

